The key part to focus on

For your interview, the most important code is actually this flow:

createBooking()
      |
      | tryLock(S1) → true
      |
      | tryLock(S2) → false
      |
      ↓
release S1
      |
      ↓
booking FAILED

And tryLock() itself is non-blocking(means if any thread is not getting lock then it does not wait and it releases all the locks it has, so here we never have a chance of deadlock)

Seat available
    → acquire
    → return true

Seat already locked
    → don't wait
    → return false

So the design handles the exact case you asked about:

User 1 → S1, S2
User 2 → S2, S3

If User 1 gets S1
and User 2 gets S2

User 1 tries S2
      ↓
false
      ↓
release S1
      ↓
User 1 booking fails

One important implementation detail: ConcurrentHashMap.compute() makes the check-and-acquire for one seat key atomic, so two threads cannot simultaneously acquire the same showId:seatId lock in the in-memory implementation.